---
import { steps } from '../data/pipeline';
import { repo } from '../data/site';
---

<section class="section" id="how">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What happens when you run it.</h2>
      <p>
        The tricky part is the end. Bun mixes readable text into the data it stores after the code, so a
        simple search for the first unreadable byte cuts too early. unbuned starts from the last file it
        is sure about and works forwards, so it can only ever leave a little too much, never eat real code.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="flow">
      {steps.map((s) => (
        <div class="plain">
          <h3>{s.title}</h3>
          <p>{s.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="cap">
      A 150 MB section is checked a chunk at a time inside Python's C code, so it stays quick even on the
      big files. There is more detail in the <a href={repo}>README</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .flow {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(290px, 1fr));
    gap: 30px 40px;
  }

  .cap {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    margin: 44px 0 0;
    max-width: 74ch;
  }
</style>
